Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'The Locked Room at the market town of Bramblewick', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Village Mysteries', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Tea and Tidings', 'text': "By noon the whole of the market town of Bramblewick was whispering. Edith Plum listened at the bakery, at the post office, at the garden gate, collecting every whisper like crumbs. Each crumb led back to a stopped watch, and a stopped watch led somewhere darker.\n\nThat evening Edith Plum spread her notes on the parlor table: a map of the market town of Bramblewick, a list of names, and a stopped watch in the center like a spider. 'Someone,' she said to the cat, 'is lying. And I mean to find out who.'\n\nBefore bed she wrote in her journal: Day one. The game is on. Tomorrow I walk the hedgerows and ask the questions no one else will ask. justice in small places — that is what this is about, though no one says so yet."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Missing Piece', 'text': "A note came then, unsigned: 'Stop asking about a stopped watch.' Edith Plum read it twice, smiled, and pinned it to her board. Threats, she told librarian Miss Faye, are confessions in a hurry.\n\nEdith Plum began with the market town of Bramblewick itself, walking every lane with a stopped watch wrapped in a handkerchief. The hedgerows of the market town of Bramblewick had seen everything, she always said; one had only to ask the right people in the right order.\n\nThat night she dreamed of the market town of Bramblewick with all its windows lit, and every window hiding something. She woke knowing the next step: confront the one person who had not been questioned — the masked visitor himself."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'Questions at Dusk', 'text': "At dusk she walked to the old vicarage alone, turning a stopped watch in her pocket. The answer, she felt, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues.\n\nBack in the parlor, Edith Plum laid out the evidence: the landlord's tale, the parish gap, the threatening note, the too-perfect description. librarian Miss Faye whistled. 'It's a noose,' they said, 'and the masked visitor tied it.'\n\nBut Edith Plum shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a stopped watch was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "The storm that had threatened the market town of Bramblewick all week broke at last, rattling the windows of the market town of Bramblewick. In the midst of it came a hammering at Edith Plum's door: librarian Miss Faye, soaked, with news — the masked visitor had been seen digging near the old vicarage.\n\nThey walked back through the storm with the evidence wrapped in oilcloth. librarian Miss Faye said nothing, but their silence was agreement. justice in small places — even on the worst night, Edith Plum thought, especially then.\n\nthe masked visitor appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Edith Plum could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned the market town of Bramblewick. 'I never meant —' began the masked visitor, and could not finish."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': 'That evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Edith Plum wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. justice in small places — I shall drink tea to that.\n\nOn market day Edith Plum laid it all before the market town of Bramblewick: a stopped watch, the letters, the photograph, the confession. The crowd in the square was silent as a church. the masked visitor stood with head bowed and did not deny a word.\n\na stopped watch was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Edith Plum declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.'}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': "librarian Miss Faye brought flowers — 'for the best detective in the county,' they said, gruff as ever. Edith Plum put them in water and told them not to be silly, but she kept the card.\n\nthe masked visitor, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Edith Plum read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nAnd if another mystery comes to the market town of Bramblewick — as mysteries do, like weather — Edith Plum will be ready, kettle on, journal open. For justice in small places, always, in the market town of Bramblewick and everywhere."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

