t, was close — as close as the church bell that began to toll, counting out the hours like clues.\n\nBut Beatrice Nettle shook her head. Something was missing — a motive with a human heart in it. a pearl button was not mere property; it was a promise, or a debt, or a love. She would find which before she accused anyone."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Storm Breaks', 'text': "the jealous heir appeared then, wild-eyed, and confessed before Beatrice Nettle could speak: it had begun as a kindness, grown into a theft, and ended in a lie that poisoned Moss Cottage. 'I never meant —' began the jealous heir, and could not finish.\n\nBeatrice Nettle listened to the end, rain running off her cape. Justice, she said quietly, was not the same as punishment; but the truth belonged to everyone in Moss Cottage, and it would be told.\n\nThey went out in rain capes, lanterns swinging, and found the disturbed earth — and beneath it, not treasure, but proof: letters, a photograph, and the mate to a pearl button. The whole sad story lay in the mud."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "That evening the kettle sang again in her cottage, and Beatrice Nettle wrote the last line in her journal: Truth told, justice done, neighbors mended. justice in small places — I shall drink tea to that.\n\na pearl button was restored to its rightful keeper, who wept to hold it. Beatrice Nettle declined all thanks, as was her custom, and slipped away before the speeches. The case was closed; the village could breathe.\n\nthe jealous heir's motive, when all heard it, drew more pity than anger — a lost inheritance, a sick child, a desperate choice. Moss Cottage was stern, but Moss Cottage was also kind; and kindness found a way to help where the law could only punish."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Quiet After', 'text': 'On Sunday Beatrice Nettle walked the hedgerows with no case at all, just the sun and the birds and the good green smell of Moss Cottage. A detective, she reflected, is only a neighbor who pays attention.\n\nthe jealous heir, from wherever the law had sent him, wrote a letter of thanks: for the truth, for the pity, for the chance to begin again. Beatrice Nettle read it twice and filed it with the case notes.\n\nAnd if another mystery comes to Moss Cottage — as mysteries do, like weather — Beatrice Nettle will be ready, kettle on, journal open. For justice in small places, always, in Moss Cottage and everywhere.'}]}

